<p id="p1">Benjamin Mayo, <a href="https://bzamayo.com/app-icons-should-be-iconic" rel="noopener noreferrer">writing about the new Music icon</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote2"><p>
  For me, a good app icon is — at its essence — memorable. An unstyled music note glyph is not memorable, and that’s exactly what Apple shipped for the new Music app icon. It’s really disappointing because I thought Apple had gotten to a good place with the state of their default app icon set; even Safari got a good icon with iOS 27.0. Then, Music in iOS 27.2 had to come along and ruin it.</p>
<p>  What is downright bizarre is that Apple introduced a rather flamboyant backstory when they launched this new brand identity for Apple Music. The campaign is “made by sound”, to use Apple’s own words. They literally <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1XxNXkOHKZw" rel="noopener noreferrer">put an acrylic rounded rectangle in a bath of oil</a>, and used sound waves to perturb the oil and capture the effect of the reverberating waves. The audio mnemonic heard in the commercials is cleverly reused to help make the visuals, too. Indeed, in the ads, you see this wave effect shimmer over the Apple Music wordmark.</p>
<p>  Yet, somehow, when it came to translating this concept into the actual image millions of people click on every day to launch the app, all of this character and flair was thrown out. There is nothing to it. It’s a black note symbol on a stock white background. <em>It’s a black note symbol on a stock white background.</em> The outgoing Music icon was simple, too, but the pinkish-red gradient background at least gave it something unique to hang off.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p3">I didn’t think it was possible that the icon of the Apple TV app – seriously, the <em>TV app</em> – could have more personality than Apple Music’s, but here we are. Makes you wonder why they didn’t use Jony Ive’s white room for <a href="https://www.applemusichall.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple Music Hall</a> instead.</p>
